WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Before you get started connecting your
ribber, turn it over and locate the slot

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.170
for the racking handle
and insert the racking handle.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.780
It's easier to visualize at this point.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:17.580
Later, you can insert and remove
the handle with the ribber attached.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.260
In the manual, this is
called an auxiliary piece.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.380
It's also known as a setting plate,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:30.080
and it holds the ribber joiner in place
to join the ribber to the main bed.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.640
Slip the setting plate
into the main bed slot as shown.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:44.960
There's a right and left auxiliary piece
or setting plate.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.820
And finger-tighten the nut,
leaving it loose.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.660
Repeat for the other side.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.940
Pick up the ribber and carefully insert

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:59.220
the joiner into the auxiliary piece
or the setting plate.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.480
You'll be inserting both sides at the same

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:06.290
time, so it might be helpful to have
an extra set of hands available.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.020
Once the ribber is in place,
tighten the screws in the setting plate.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:13.400
Position the ribber joiner support leg

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.210
against your table
and secure it with a clamp.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.490
Repeat this for the other side.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.920
Next, rotate the pushup cam

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:26.620
in the direction of the arrow and be
sure that it clicks into place.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:29.700
This provides extra support for the ribber.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.260
Using both hands,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.400
raise the ribber all the way up and confirm
that the connections are tight.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.280
Next, you'll join the knitting
carriages and explore using your ribber.

